  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDry conditions will persist through this morning, with \nprecipitation developing from south to north this afternoon \ninto early evening associated with a deepening upper-990s mb \ncyclone tracking northward across the mid Atlantic region today.\nA short wave aloft, isentropic lift and low level F-Gen will \nprovide strong forcing resulting in a large area of mainly rain,\nwith most falling in 6-10 hour period. Main change from prior \nforecasts is slightly delayed arrival and exit of precip. An \nintensifying low-level E-SE jet will bring a significant \nincrease in deep layer moisture, with NAEFs forecasting \nintegrated water vapor anomalies of +2 to +4 STDEV resulting in \nPWAT anomalies surging to +2 to +3 STDEV this afternoon into \nthis evening. Rain will fall moderate to heavy at times, \nespecially during the evening to early overnight hours. Total \nQPF remains similar, although differences between low/high end \namounts are more pronounced due to upslope/downslope factors \nranging from 0.50\" to 1.75\". Greatest amounts E. Catskills, \nwestern parts of Mid Hudson Valley and NW CT. Probabilities for\n> 2.00\" of rain are 40-80% for limited areas where upslope is \nfavored in NW CT and E. Catskills. Given dry antecedent \nconditions and lack of rain for the past two weeks, flooding is \nnot anticipated although ponding of water may occur especially \nin urban and poor drainage locations. Minor tidal flooding may \noccur along the Hudson River at Poughkeepsie during the tidal \ncycles this afternoon and tonight, but confidence is low. The \nrain should taper off to showers overnight into the pre-dawn \nhours Fri, as dry slot moves in from the south. Additional \nshowers are expected on Fri, as the main core of the upper low \ngradually moves in from the south/west. \n\nWinter Weather Advisory unchanged for parts of the S. \nAdirondacks and S. Greens for some light icing due to freezing \nrain in sheltered areas there. Still expecting surface \ntemperatures to be near 32F at precip onset and for a few hours \nafter through the evening, with a pronounced warm nose aloft \ndeveloping. Isolated spots in the E. Catskills could also see a \nbrief period of freezing rain at onset, but should quickly turn \nover the plain rain. Where the freezing rain occurs, radial ice \naccumulation in these limited areas should be mainly a few \nhundredths to < 0.10\". Freezing rain will change to plain rain \novernight as surface temperatures warm.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWind Advisory remains in effect for the higher terrain of W. \nNew England, E. Catskills and W. Mohawk Valley. As the storm \nsystem approaches from the south today, easterly winds will \nincrease substantially through the day into this evening. NAEFs \nindicating anomalously high 850 mb easterly (u-component) 850 mb\nwind anomalies of -4 to -6 STDEV developing later this \nafternoon into this evening. Deterministic guidance from the \nGFS/RRFS showing a 60-80 kt jet at 850 mb. The greatest winds \nwill likely not fully mix down due to a strengthening low level \ninversion developing, however winds of 40-50 kt are displaced \njust beneath the inversion. Strongest winds look to occur during\nonset of rain for a few hours while the low levels are still \nrelatively dry, which is during the evening. Based on forecast \nsoundings and rising probabilities for > 50 mph gusts, \nincreased winds in Bennington, Berkshire and E. Rensselaer \ncounties to mention 50-55 mph peak gusts this evening, with \n40-50 mph in the rest of the Wind Advisory area. Gusts of 25-40 \nmph will be common elsewhere. Winds will gradually weaken \novernight into early Fri morning, as the low level jet shifts \nnorth of the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nThe next system approaching from the west, looks to bring \nanother round of mainly rain Sun night into Mon. Guidance \nshowing a progressive trough aloft, with a surface cyclone \npassing by north/west of our area, with a trailing cold front\nmoving east across the region. Due to quick movement and\nrelatively limited moisture, rainfall amounts will be less than\ntoday's system. NBM probabilities for > 0.50\" rain are only \n15-60%, with the highest chances in favored upslope areas of the\nSW. Adirondacks and S. Greens. Temperatures should be mild \nenough for plain rain, except for some brief snow showers in \nparts of the Adirondacks later Mon as colder air may briefly \nfilter in prior to precip ending.",
